From c624374a00800e92245277aa56f0569fb8b8a19e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rafe Walker <45343084+rafe-walker@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 14:00:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 12/17] feat(mcp): add batch tools for insights and annotations

Add insights-batch-update, annotations-batch-create and
annotations-batch-delete as hand-written tools under src/tools/atomic/.
Job 3 of the 2026-11-10 friction session (tag 10 insights, archive 5,
add 8 annotations) took 25 calls; job 10's annotation and insight
cleanup took 23. Each is now one call per object type.

The shared engine (batch.ts) gives every batch the same contract:
- every item is validated first (input, the object exists, what would
  change); if any item fails, nothing is written and each item says why
- writes then run best-effort, four at a time, through ApiClient (which
  keeps its 429 / Retry-After handling); a failed write is reported on
  its item and the others are kept
- one result per item (ok, unchanged, error, conflict, skipped,
  not_attempted, exists, not_found) with a one-line `changed` receipt,
  and a summary that says atomic: false
- dry_run validates and reports without writing; stop_on_error starts
  no new writes after the first failure; at most 50 items per call

insights-batch-update edits name, description, favorited and tags, or
soft-deletes (insights have no archive state). add_tags/remove_tags are
a read-merge-write, so those items go through the version guard
(versionGuard.ts): the insight is re-read just before the write and an
item whose insight changed since validation reports `conflict` and is
not written.

annotations-batch-create validates each item with annotation-create's
own schema and is retry-safe: an item matching a live annotation
(content, date_marker, scope) is reported `exists` instead of being
created twice. annotations-batch-delete soft-deletes and reports a
missing id as not_found without blocking the rest.

Tests use an in-memory fake of the REST endpoints with jittered latency.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
